<header class="sp-head"><p class="featured-region">14 August 1947 · Constituent Assembly, New Delhi</p><h1 class="sp-title">Nehru: Tryst with Destiny</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>On the eve of independence from British rule, Jawaharlal Nehru, about to become India's first prime minister, addressed the Constituent Assembly as midnight approached.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>At the stroke of the midnight hour, when the world sleeps, India will awake to life and freedom.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Speech to the Constituent Assembly, 14–15 August 1947.</p><div class="sp-full"><a class="sp-full-link" href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/A_Tryst_With_Destiny" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the complete text<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a><span class="sp-full-src">Wikisource</span></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>India became independent at midnight, but the partition that created Pakistan brought mass migration and communal violence in which hundreds of thousands died. Nehru led India until his death in 1964.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/republic-of-india">Republic of India</a></div></div></section>
